T26.01|Acquisition rationale explains why ownership of another business should create more value than the alternatives. Potential benefits include capabilities, distribution, cost savings and faster market access. Size alone is not a value-creation argument: the buyer must compare benefits with purchase price, integration costs and risk.~~Separate value created for the combined business from value captured by the buyer. Paying the seller all anticipated benefits can leave buyer shareholders no gain.|Operating rationale: Improves revenues, costs or capabilities.;;Financial rationale: Changes financing or capital allocation.;;Strategic rationale: Changes competitive position or options.|A target's standalone value is ₹100 lakh, credible net synergy value ₹20, and asking price ₹125.~~Combined value contribution is ₹120.;;Paying ₹125 exceeds that estimate by ₹5.;;Revisit synergies, price or alternatives.~~A strategically appealing transaction can destroy buyer value.~~Estimates must reflect execution risk and avoid double-counting growth.|State the causal benefit.;;Value alternatives.;;Compare price with risk-adjusted incremental value.
T26.02|Horizontal acquisitions combine businesses at a similar production stage; vertical acquisitions connect supply-chain stages; conglomerate acquisitions combine different activities. The classification helps identify possible synergies, competition concerns and integration demands, but does not determine whether a deal is attractive.~~Vertical ownership can improve coordination while sacrificing supplier flexibility. Diversification at company level may be costly when shareholders can diversify more cheaply themselves.|Horizontal: Combines competitors or peers.;;Vertical: Integrates supplier or customer stages.;;Conglomerate: Combines different business lines.|A manufacturer pays a supplier ₹30 lakh yearly and considers buying it for ₹100.~~Estimate genuinely avoidable external margin, not all purchases.;;Include operating and investment costs that remain.;;Evaluate supply reliability and loss of sourcing flexibility.~~Internalising purchases does not eliminate their economic cost.~~Competition and transaction rules require jurisdiction-specific review.|Classify the relationship.;;Identify costs that really disappear.;;Assess integration and competition constraints.
T26.03|A sell-side process markets a business and seeks suitable offers; a buy-side process identifies targets and evaluates acquisition opportunities. Preparation, information access, negotiations, due diligence and approvals lead toward signing and completion, which are distinct events.~~Competitive tension can improve terms, but confidential information and execution certainty need careful control. A signed agreement may still depend on financing, approvals or other closing conditions.|Auction: Multiple buyers compete through staged bids.;;Bilateral process: One buyer and seller negotiate.;;Signing/closing: Contract agreement versus completed transfer.|A seller receives bids of ₹105 and ₹110 lakh; the higher bidder has uncertain financing.~~Compare cash price and conditions.;;Assess probability and timing of completion.;;Negotiate protections and a credible closing path.~~The highest headline offer may not be the best executable outcome.~~Contract remedies depend on the actual agreement and governing law.|Prepare reliable information.;;Track conditions and approvals.;;Compare certainty with price.
T26.04|Target screening narrows acquisition candidates against explicit strategic and financial criteria. Criteria might include capabilities, geography, scale, profitability, capital needs and cultural compatibility. A screen is an initial filter rather than a substitute for diligence or valuation.~~Weight criteria according to the acquisition thesis. Attractive margins alone can conceal customer concentration, obsolete technology or future investment requirements.|Strategic fit: Supports the intended capability or market.;;Financial fit: Cash generation, funding and valuation.;;Execution fit: Integration feasibility and management readiness.|Three targets have EBITDA of ₹10 lakh each, but one requires ₹8 recurring capex and another ₹2.~~Compare cash conversion rather than EBITDA alone.;;Check whether investment supports maintenance or growth.;;Shortlist based on thesis and remaining risks.~~Equal EBITDA does not imply equal cash value.~~A preliminary screen needs subsequent verification.|Define criteria before reviewing names.;;Use consistent data.;;Record reasons for inclusion and exclusion.
T26.05|Due diligence tests the assumptions behind a transaction. Financial review examines earnings, cash and debt-like items; commercial review tests markets and customers; tax and legal work examines obligations, rights and enforceability. Findings should change valuation, terms or the decision.~~Diligence cannot eliminate every uncertainty. Prioritise material issues and track how each is treated, rather than collecting documents without resolving their economic significance.|Financial: Quality of earnings and balance-sheet obligations.;;Commercial: Demand, competition and customer durability.;;Tax/legal: Liabilities, ownership and contractual rights.|Reported EBITDA is ₹20 lakh, including ₹4 non-recurring income; an undisclosed obligation is ₹6.~~Normalised EBITDA is ₹16.;;At an assumed 8× multiple, operating value changes from ₹160 to ₹128.;;Treat the obligation in the agreed equity bridge.~~Verified facts can change both valuation and transaction terms.~~The multiple is illustrative, and tax/legal conclusions require specialists.|Reconcile earnings to cash.;;Verify major rights and liabilities.;;Link findings to price, protection or rejection.
T26.06|Transaction valuation estimates the price a buyer can justify for the specific ownership rights and cash flows acquired. Standalone value, control opportunities, buyer-specific synergies and execution costs may differ from public trading valuations. Control does not automatically justify a fixed premium.~~Use multiple methods and an explicit enterprise-to-equity bridge. The seller's price, lender's capacity and buyer's value estimate answer different questions.|Standalone value: Business without buyer-specific benefits.;;Strategic value: Includes incremental buyer opportunities.;;Equity purchase value: Enterprise value adjusted for claims and cash.|Target EV is ₹150 lakh, debt ₹40, excess cash ₹10; net buyer synergy value is ₹20.~~Standalone equity value is ₹120.;;Maximum equity value on these assumptions is ₹140.;;Leave room for estimation risk when negotiating.~~Purchase value depends on rights and the equity bridge.~~Maximum model value is not automatically a prudent bid.|Identify what is acquired.;;Use consistent cash/debt definitions.;;Stress synergy and financing assumptions.
T26.07|Synergies are benefits achievable through combination; dis-synergies are incremental harms such as customer loss, disruption or higher complexity. Revenue synergies usually require adoption and investment, while cost savings may require severance or systems spending. Timing and probability matter to present value.~~Avoid counting a benefit already embedded in the standalone forecast. Track actual cash savings rather than merely announced run-rate targets.|Cost synergy: Eliminates duplicated or inefficient spending.;;Revenue synergy: Adds sales or improves pricing.;;Dis-synergy: Offsets value through disruption or lost business.|A merger promises ₹5 lakh annual savings but requires ₹8 upfront integration and loses ₹2 contribution annually during transition.~~Initial net annual benefit is ₹3.;;Model the ₹8 cash outflow separately.;;Discount benefits over a realistic implementation schedule.~~Headline savings overstate early net value.~~Duration, taxes and execution probability determine total value.|Remove double counting.;;Include integration costs and losses.;;Assign milestones and accountable owners.
T26.08|Cash consideration fixes a monetary payment, while stock consideration gives sellers ownership in the combined company. Mixed deals combine the two. Fixed-share and fixed-value exchange terms distribute market-price risk differently between buyer and seller.~~Consideration affects financing, dilution and risk sharing; it does not itself establish whether the purchase price is fair.|Cash: Seller receives money at agreed terms.;;Fixed-share stock: Share count fixed, value fluctuates.;;Fixed-value stock: Target value fixed through a pricing formula.|A deal offers 2 buyer shares for each target share; buyer price is ₹50 initially and ₹40 at completion.~~Initial implied value is ₹100 per target share.;;Completion value is ₹80 if the ratio stays fixed.;;Compare with a fixed-value or collar arrangement.~~Exchange-ratio design allocates price risk.~~Actual agreements may impose averaging periods or limits.|Read exchange mechanics.;;Model ownership dilution.;;Assess financing and price-risk allocation.
T26.09|Acquisition financing may use cash reserves, debt, new equity or a combination. Funding choice changes liquidity, interest burden, ownership and refinancing exposure. A financing package should support downside operations and integration rather than only the base-case purchase.~~Debt capacity follows sustainable cash available for service and contractual constraints. Cash on the balance sheet may be restricted or needed for the existing business.|Internal cash: Uses available company liquidity.;;Acquisition debt: Adds fixed financing obligations.;;New equity: Shares risk while diluting existing ownership.|Purchase funding requires ₹100 lakh; usable cash is ₹20, new debt ₹50 and equity ₹30.~~Sources total ₹100.;;At assumed 10% interest, new annual interest is ₹5.;;Test post-integration cash generation against interest and principal.~~A balanced sources-and-uses table is necessary but insufficient.~~Fees, minimum cash and refinancing create further needs.|Separate usable and operating cash.;;Include fees in uses.;;Stress debt service and covenant headroom.
T26.10|Accretion means a transaction raises a selected per-share metric, often EPS; dilution means it lowers that metric. Financing, accounting, tax and share issuance affect the calculation. EPS accretion is not proof of positive net present value or sustainable cash returns.~~Compare operating value and invested capital as well as EPS. Cheap debt can make an overpriced acquisition look accretive before its risks emerge.|EPS accretion: Combined earnings per share rises.;;EPS dilution: Combined earnings per share falls.;;Economic value: Incremental benefits exceed investment and risk costs.|Buyer earns ₹100 lakh on 100 lakh shares; target adds ₹20 after incremental costs, and 10 lakh shares are issued.~~Old EPS is ₹1.;;New EPS is ₹120 ÷ 110 = ₹1.091.;;Evaluate price and cash economics separately.~~EPS rises about 9.1% under the assumptions.~~Purchase accounting and recurring financing must be included consistently.|Use fully diluted shares.;;Reconcile incremental costs.;;Keep accretion separate from value creation.
T26.11|A merger model combines standalone forecasts, transaction sources and uses, purchase accounting, financing and synergies. It tests pro forma statements, ownership and per-share effects. Models should distinguish recurring operating changes from one-time integration or accounting items.~~The opening balance sheet must reflect the actual transaction structure. A plug that makes it balance can hide missing funding or an incorrectly treated liability.|Sources and uses: Reconciles funding with transaction needs.;;Pro forma statements: Combined post-transaction accounts.;;Purchase allocation: Recognises acquired assets and obligations.|Buyer borrows ₹60 lakh and issues ₹40 equity to pay ₹95 price and ₹5 fees.~~Funding and uses both equal ₹100.;;Add debt and equity to the combined financing schedules.;;Apply acquired balance-sheet and purchase-allocation assumptions.~~The model connects transaction mechanics to future earnings and cash.~~Fee and goodwill treatment depends on applicable accounting rules.|Reconcile sources and uses.;;Separate one-time and recurring items.;;Check cash, debt and opening balances.
T26.12|Purchase-price mechanisms allocate the effect of changes between a reference date and completion. Completion accounts adjust agreed items using closing measurements; a locked-box uses a reference balance sheet with protections against specified value leakage. Normal working-capital targets prevent a seller delivering an underfunded operating business.~~Definitions matter more than labels. Cash, debt, debt-like obligations and normal working capital must avoid overlap.|Completion accounts: Closing-date price adjustments.;;Locked-box: Reference-date price with leakage rules.;;Working-capital adjustment: Difference from agreed normal level.|Agreed equity price is ₹100 lakh with normal working capital ₹20; delivered working capital is ₹16.~~Shortfall is ₹4.;;Under an assumed rupee-for-rupee adjustment, price becomes ₹96.;;Verify definitions and permitted seasonal variations.~~Operating funding can change effective purchase cost.~~Actual adjustment rights follow the negotiated agreement.|Define each adjustment item.;;Use normal seasonal levels.;;Avoid double-counting debt-like balances.
T26.13|An earn-out makes part of consideration depend on future performance or events. It can bridge valuation disagreement, but measurement, operating control and incentives may create disputes. Accounting revenue, EBITDA and cash receipts can produce very different payouts.~~Specify who controls decisions affecting the metric and how exceptional items are handled. An earn-out is not simply an optimistic forecast added to price.|Revenue earn-out: Payout tied to sales.;;Profit earn-out: Payout tied to an earnings metric.;;Milestone earn-out: Payout tied to a defined event.|Seller receives ₹80 lakh upfront plus ₹20 if year-two EBITDA exceeds ₹15.~~At ₹14 EBITDA, total payment is ₹80.;;At ₹16, it is ₹100 under the binary rule.;;Test incentive effects near the threshold.~~Contingent consideration shares outcome risk.~~Contracts may use graduated formulas and specific accounting policies.|Define the metric precisely.;;Address operating-control conflicts.;;Model payout scenarios and dispute rules.
T26.14|A leveraged buyout finances an acquisition with substantial debt supported by business cash flows. A management buyout involves the management team purchasing the business and may or may not use high leverage. Equity returns depend on operating outcomes, debt reduction and exit value.~~Debt increases sensitivity to downturns and can constrain necessary investment. Attractive model IRR should be tested against financing viability and downside recoveries.|LBO: Acquisition with significant debt financing.;;MBO: Acquisition involving incumbent management.;;Sponsor equity: Residual capital bearing leveraged risk.|Entry EV is ₹100 lakh funded with ₹70 debt and ₹30 equity; exit EV is ₹110 and remaining debt ₹40.~~Exit equity is ₹70.;;Equity multiple is 70 ÷ 30 = 2.33×.;;If exit EV is ₹65, equity is only ₹25.~~Debt repayment and exit value jointly drive results.~~Timing, fees and cash distributions determine IRR.|Stress operating cash and refinancing.;;Explain each return driver.;;Include investment needs and covenant limits.
T26.15|Divestitures sell assets or businesses; carve-outs separate a business for sale or listing; spin-offs distribute ownership to existing shareholders; split-offs exchange parent shares for subsidiary shares under specified terms. Separation can improve focus but introduces stranded costs and operational dependencies.~~A business's reported segment profit may exclude services it will need to buy after separation. Transitional agreements and standalone capital needs affect value.|Sale: Transfers ownership for consideration.;;Spin-off: Distributes subsidiary shares.;;Carve-out/split-off: Partial separation or share exchange.|A division reports ₹15 lakh profit, but standalone IT and corporate functions cost another ₹4.~~Standalone profit is ₹11 before other changes.;;Identify parent costs that remain after separation.;;Evaluate proceeds and both companies' funding.~~Segment profitability overstates standalone earnings if shared costs are omitted.~~Legal and tax structure varies by jurisdiction.|Estimate standalone costs.;;Identify stranded parent costs.;;Plan service and financing transitions.
T26.16|Takeover defences influence how control can be acquired; activism seeks changes in strategy, capital allocation or governance. Their effect on value depends on whether they protect long-term bargaining power or entrench management. Rules and permitted mechanisms vary materially by market.~~Assess rights and incentives rather than assuming every defence or activist proposal benefits shareholders.|Governance defence: Changes control procedures within local rules.;;Activist engagement: Seeks strategic or financial changes.;;Control contest: Competing proposals for ownership or direction.|An activist proposes distributing ₹30 lakh cash, but the company needs ₹20 for essential renewal and ₹15 liquidity.~~Required resources total ₹35.;;The proposed distribution creates a ₹5 shortfall before other funding.;;Compare alternative plans and operating evidence.~~A superficially attractive payout can impair resilience.~~The example does not imply a legal right to force the distribution.|Review actual shareholder rights.;;Test proposed economics.;;Assess conflicts and long-term investment.
T26.17|Financial restructuring changes debt, equity or payment obligations to restore viability or redistribute claims. Debt exchanges can extend maturity, reduce principal, change interest or convert claims into equity. These changes do not cure weak operating economics by themselves.~~Compare the going-concern plan with available alternatives and creditor priorities. Negotiation depends on rights, consent rules and credible funding.|Maturity extension: Delays repayment.;;Principal reduction: Reduces contractual amount.;;Debt-for-equity swap: Exchanges debt claims for ownership.|A company owes ₹100 lakh but a supported recovery plan can service only ₹70.~~Identify a ₹30 funding or claim gap.;;Model an assumed ₹30 debt-to-equity conversion.;;Test whether remaining debt is sustainable.~~Restructuring changes claims and ownership, not necessarily enterprise value.~~Accounting and creditor consent depend on the actual framework.|Establish operating viability.;;Model claim alternatives.;;Review consent and enforcement requirements.
T26.18|Insolvency analysis maps assets, claims and legally applicable priorities. Secured rights, administrative costs and different creditor classes affect recoveries; shareholders are generally residual claimants. Exact priority and enforcement depend on jurisdiction and the specific security.~~A simplified waterfall is an analytical model, not universal legal advice. Costs, delays and competing liens can reduce the cash available.|Secured claim: Has specified collateral rights.;;Unsecured claim: Relies on remaining estate and legal priority.;;Residual equity: Receives value after superior claims.|Assume ₹80 lakh distributable value, a valid first-ranking ₹50 claim, then ₹40 junior debt, with no other costs.~~Pay ₹50 to the first-ranking claim.;;Junior debt receives ₹30, or 75% recovery.;;Equity receives zero.~~Enterprise recovery and each class's recovery differ.~~Actual law, collateral and expenses can change the order.|Verify legal priority.;;Deduct costs and delays.;;Model recovery by class rather than company average.
T26.19|Post-merger integration converts the transaction thesis into operating actions. Value tracking compares actual benefits, costs and business performance with the investment case. A reported synergy target should have a baseline, owner, timing and cash consequence.~~Separate merger effects from market changes and standalone improvements. Integration that damages customer service or staff retention can offset apparent savings.|Integration plan: Sequenced operational and cultural work.;;Synergy register: Benefits with owners and measurement rules.;;Value review: Compares total outcomes with the acquisition case.|A deal promised ₹6 lakh recurring savings; verified savings are ₹4, integration cost ₹5 and lost contribution ₹2 in year one.~~Year-one net effect is ₹4 − ₹5 − ₹2 = −₹3.;;Track whether savings recur and losses recover.;;Update the remaining value forecast.~~Achievement must be measured net of implementation damage.~~A first-year loss alone does not settle lifetime value.|Set baselines before closing.;;Track cash and customer outcomes.;;Update forecasts with verified results.
